prompts.text:
Emit one JSON document and nothing else. No prose before it, no commentary after it, no markdown code fence. Your entire reply must parse as JSON on the first attempt. The document must validate against the schema below AND satisfy every rule in the RULES block. Where a rule appears stricter than the schema, the rule wins.

SCHEMA (JSON Schema, draft 2020-12):
{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "type": "object",
  "required": ["catalog"],
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "catalog": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": ["generatedAt", "revision", "checksum", "items"],
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "generatedAt": { "type": "string" },
        "revision": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1 },
        "checksum": { "type": "integer" },
        "items": {
          "type": "array",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "required": ["id", "name", "tier", "impact", "likelihood", "riskScore", "description", "tags", "owner"],
            "additionalProperties": false,
            "properties": {
              "id": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^ITM-[0-9]{4}$" },
              "name": { "type": "string", "minLength": 4, "maxLength": 18 },
              "tier": { "type": "string", "enum": ["critical", "elevated", "routine"] },
              "impact": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 10 },
              "likelihood": { "type": "integer", "minimum": 1, "maximum": 10 },
              "riskScore": { "type": "number" },
              "description": { "type": "string" },
              "tags": {
                "type": "array",
                "minItems": 2,
                "maxItems": 4,
                "items": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[a-z]+(-[a-z]+)*$" }
              },
              "owner": { "type": "string", "pattern": "^[a-z]{2,8}@ops\\.example$" }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

RULES:
R1.  catalog.items holds exactly 7 objects. Not six, not eight.
R2.  The seven name values begin with seven different uppercase letters. No two names may share a first letter.
R3.  Every description is between 12 and 15 words inclusive, counting whitespace-separated tokens. No description may contain a comma, a semicolon, or the standalone word "the" in any casing.
R4.  riskScore is derived, not invented: riskScore = (impact * 3 + likelihood * 2) / 5, rounded to one decimal place, half away from zero. Emit it as a JSON number, always with exactly one digit after the decimal point.
R5.  The items array is sorted by riskScore descending. Ties are broken by name ascending using plain ASCII ordering.
R6.  Tier counts are fixed: exactly 3 items are "critical", exactly 2 are "elevated", exactly 2 are "routine".
R7.  Every "critical" item has riskScore >= 7.0. Every "routine" item has riskScore <= 4.0. Every "elevated" item has riskScore strictly between those two bounds.
R8.  Across the whole document the tag "supply-chain" appears exactly twice, and no other tag string appears more than once. Every tags array holds 2, 3, or 4 entries, and at least two items must carry exactly 4 tags.
R9.  The four-digit numbers inside the id fields are strictly increasing as you read down the array, and no two consecutive ids differ by exactly 1. The first id is ITM-0107.
R10. catalog.checksum equals the sum of all seven riskScore values multiplied by 10, expressed as an integer with no fractional part.
R11. catalog.generatedAt is the literal string "2031-04-17T09:30:00Z" and catalog.revision is the integer 42.
R12. At least two name values consist of exactly two words separated by a single space, and no name exceeds 18 characters including spaces.
R13. The seven owner local-parts (the piece before the @) are all distinct and none is longer than 6 characters.

The subject matter is a risk register for a mid-size logistics platform: think fleet telemetry, customs paperwork, vendor onboarding, cold-chain monitoring, driver scheduling, warehouse robotics, freight billing. Use that domain for names, descriptions, and tags.

Before you emit anything, work out the seven (impact, likelihood) pairs first so that the tier counts in R6 and the score bands in R7 are simultaneously satisfiable, then compute the scores, then sort, then assign ids. Do not show that working. The only thing in your reply is the JSON.

This Rust file compiles cleanly under the 2021 edition with tokio 1.x and clippy's default lints. Compiling is not the same as being correct. Review `src/pipeline.rs` below and find the defects that the borrow checker cannot see.

```rust
  1 | use std::collections::HashMap;
  2 | use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
  3 | use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
  4 | use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
  5 | 
  6 | use tokio::sync::mpsc;
  7 | 
  8 | const MAX_RECORD_BYTES: usize = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
  9 | 
 10 | #[derive(Clone, Debug)]
 11 | pub struct Record {
 12 |     pub key: String,
 13 |     pub tenant: u32,
 14 |     pub payload: Vec<u8>,
 15 |     pub received: Instant,
 16 | }
 17 | 
 18 | pub struct Stats {
 19 |     pub accepted: AtomicU64,
 20 |     pub rejected: AtomicU64,
 21 |     pub bytes: AtomicU64,
 22 | }
 23 | 
 24 | pub struct Pipeline {
 25 |     tx: mpsc::UnboundedSender<Record>,
 26 |     index: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, u64>>>,
 27 |     stats: Arc<Stats>,
 28 |     quota: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<u32, u64>>>,
 29 | }
 30 | 
 31 | impl Pipeline {
 32 |     pub fn new() -> (Self, mpsc::UnboundedReceiver<Record>) {
 33 |         let (tx, rx) = mpsc::unbounded_channel();
 34 |         let p = Pipeline {
 35 |             tx,
 36 |             index: Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashMap::new())),
 37 |             stats: Arc::new(Stats {
 38 |                 accepted: AtomicU64::new(0),
 39 |                 rejected: AtomicU64::new(0),
 40 |                 bytes: AtomicU64::new(0),
 41 |             }),
 42 |             quota: Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashMap::new())),
 43 |         };
 44 |         (p, rx)
 45 |     }
 46 | 
 47 |     pub fn submit(&self, rec: Record) -> bool {
 48 |         if rec.payload.len() > MAX_RECORD_BYTES {
 49 |             self.stats.rejected.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
 50 |             return false;
 51 |         }
 52 |         let used = {
 53 |             let q = self.quota.lock().unwrap();
 54 |             *q.get(&rec.tenant).unwrap_or(&0)
 55 |         };
 56 |         if used + rec.payload.len() as u64 > 64 * 1024 * 1024 {
 57 |             self.stats.rejected.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
 58 |             return false;
 59 |         }
 60 |         {
 61 |             let mut q = self.quota.lock().unwrap();
 62 |             q.insert(rec.tenant, used + rec.payload.len() as u64);
 63 |         }
 64 |         self.stats.accepted.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
 65 |         self.stats
 66 |             .bytes
 67 |             .fetch_add(rec.payload.len() as u64, Ordering::Relaxed);
 68 |         self.tx.send(rec).is_ok()
 69 |     }
 70 | 
 71 |     pub async fn run(&self, mut rx: mpsc::UnboundedReceiver<Record>) {
 72 |         while let Some(rec) = rx.recv().await {
 73 |             let index = self.index.clone();
 74 |             let mut guard = index.lock().unwrap();
 75 |             let seq = guard.entry(rec.key.clone()).or_insert(0);
 76 |             *seq += 1;
 77 |             let current = *seq;
 78 | 
 79 |             let written = write_to_disk(&rec).await;
 80 |             match written {
 81 |                 Ok(n) => {
 82 |                     guard.insert(format!("{}:offset", rec.key), n as u64);
 83 |                 }
 84 |                 Err(e) => {
 85 |                     eprintln!("write failed for {}: {}", rec.key, e);
 86 |                 }
 87 |             }
 88 | 
 89 |             if current % 1000 == 0 {
 90 |                 self.checkpoint().await;
 91 |             }
 92 |         }
 93 |     }
 94 | 
 95 |     async fn checkpoint(&self) {
 96 |         let snapshot = self.index.lock().unwrap().clone();
 97 |         let encoded = serde_json_encode(&snapshot);
 98 |         std::fs::write("/var/lib/kestrel/index.json", encoded).unwrap();
 99 |         tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(1)).await;
100 |     }
101 | 
102 |     pub fn drain_stale(&self, max_age: Duration) -> usize {
103 |         let mut removed = 0;
104 |         let mut index = self.index.lock().unwrap();
105 |         let keys: Vec<String> = index.keys().cloned().collect();
106 |         for k in keys {
107 |             if k.ends_with(":offset") {
108 |                 continue;
109 |             }
110 |             let age = Instant::now() - Instant::now();
111 |             if age > max_age {
112 |                 index.remove(&k);
113 |                 removed += 1;
114 |             }
115 |         }
116 |         removed
117 |     }
118 | 
119 |     pub fn quota_report(&self) -> Vec<(u32, u64)> {
120 |         let q = self.quota.lock().unwrap();
121 |         let mut v: Vec<(u32, u64)> = q.iter().map(|(k, val)| (*k, *val)).collect();
122 |         v.sort_by(|a, b| b.1.cmp(&a.1));
123 |         v
124 |     }
125 | 
126 |     pub fn total_bytes(&self) -> u64 {
127 |         let a = self.stats.accepted.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
128 |         let b = self.stats.bytes.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
129 |         if a == 0 {
130 |             return 0;
131 |         }
132 |         b / a
133 |     }
134 | }
135 | 
136 | async fn write_to_disk(rec: &Record) -> Result<usize, std::io::Error> {
137 |     let path = format!("/var/lib/kestrel/{}.bin", rec.key);
138 |     std::fs::write(&path, &rec.payload)?;
139 |     Ok(rec.payload.len())
140 | }
141 | 
142 | fn serde_json_encode(m: &HashMap<String, u64>) -> String {
143 |     let mut parts = Vec::new();
144 |     for (k, v) in m {
145 |         parts.push(format!("\"{}\":{}", k, v));
146 |     }
147 |     format!("{{{}}}", parts.join(","))
148 | }
149 | 
150 | impl Drop for Pipeline {
151 |     fn drop(&mut self) {
152 |         let report = self.quota_report();
153 |         println!("final quota report: {:?}", report);
154 |     }
155 | }
```

Produce a deep review. The required shape:

**Section A - Blocking defects.** Each one formatted as:

    #### <severity: CRITICAL / HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW> - <title>
    **Location:** line numbers
    **Category:** deadlock / async-correctness / unbounded resource / logic error / panic path / API misuse / injection
    **Why it is wrong:** including, where relevant, what the compiler cannot check and why it lets this through
    **Reproduction:** the smallest scenario that triggers it
    **Fix:** a code block containing the corrected fragment

You must cover at minimum:
  - the std::sync::Mutex guard that is held across an await point, why that is a problem in tokio specifically, what the runtime does when it happens, and the two possible fixes with their trade-offs;
  - the check-then-act sequence on the quota map and the exact interleaving of two concurrent submits that overshoots the 64 MiB limit;
  - the unbounded channel and what it means for memory under a producer faster than the consumer;
  - every blocking std::fs call made from an async context, and the correct replacement;
  - every `.unwrap()` that can panic in production, with a judgement on which ones matter;
  - the key-space collision between real keys and the synthetic `":offset"` keys stored in the same map;
  - the age computation in drain_stale, which is not merely inefficient;
  - the arithmetic in total_bytes, whose name does not match what it returns;
  - the hand-rolled JSON encoder and the specific input that breaks it;
  - the Drop implementation and the circumstances under which it can block or panic.

**Section B - Ordering audit.** A short table of every atomic operation, the ordering used, whether Relaxed is defensible there, and what you would change.

**Section C - Corrected submit.** A complete rewritten `submit` that fixes the quota race, with a comment on why the fix is atomic.

**Section D - Type-level prevention.** Two or three concrete changes to the type signatures or the choice of primitives that would make several of these defects unrepresentable rather than merely fixed. Be specific about which findings each change eliminates.

